#: ../../../development/releases.rst:3
msgid "Release Process"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../development/releases.rst:5
msgid ""
"In order to allow for rapid, predictable releases, Setuptools uses a "
"mechanical technique for releases, enacted on tagged commits by continuous "
"integration."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../development/releases.rst:9
msgid ""
"To finalize a release, run ``tox -e finalize``, review, then push the "
"changes."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../development/releases.rst:12
msgid "If tests pass, the release will be uploaded to PyPI."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../development/releases.rst:15
msgid "Release Frequency"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../development/releases.rst:17
msgid ""
"Some have asked why Setuptools is released so frequently. Because Setuptools "
"uses a mechanical release process, it's very easy to make releases whenever "
"the code is stable (tests are passing). As a result, the philosophy is to "
"release early and often."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../development/releases.rst:22
msgid ""
"While some find the frequent releases somewhat surprising, they only empower "
"the user. Although releases are made frequently, users can choose the "
"frequency at which they use those releases. If instead Setuptools "
"contributions were only released in batches, the user would be constrained "
"to only use Setuptools when those official releases were made. With frequent "
"releases, the user can govern exactly how often he wishes to update."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../development/releases.rst:29
msgid ""
"Frequent releases also then obviate the need for dev or beta releases in "
"most cases. Because releases are made early and often, bugs are discovered "
"and corrected quickly, in many cases before other users have yet to "
"encounter them."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../development/releases.rst:34
msgid "Release Managers"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../development/releases.rst:36
msgid ""
"Additionally, anyone with push access to the master branch has access to cut "
"releases."
msgstr ""
